+### Inversion Detection
+A method to detect if a triangle is inverted (facing the inside of the object) by checking the dot product of the triangle normal and the field function gradients at its vertices [S2, unofficial].
+**Formula**
+$n \cdot F'(a) > 0 \text{ AND } n \cdot F'(b) > 0 \text{ AND } n \cdot F'(c) > 0$ [S2, unofficial].
+**Pitfalls**
+Discrete integration can let points cross an edge and create a fold [S2, unofficial]. Edge collapsing may invert a neighboring triangle [S2, unofficial]. Surface projection constraints may invert triangles when projecting points to the surface [S2, unofficial]. Edge subdivision inherently inserts new inverted elements when subdividing an edge belonging to an inverted triangle [S2, unofficial]. User interaction or surface animation may modify particle positions too fast for the mechanical system to follow them [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originating from sculpting applications requiring precise topological queries, this family was detailed in "Detail sculpting using cubical marching squares" [S1]. The methods allow for efficient visual quality improvements in sculpting by providing robust inside/outside tests for vertices and samples. The use of cubical marching squares and associated data structures (like BVHs and 2D grids) optimizes the intersection search for these topological queries [S1].
+The methods discussed in [S1] provide better visual quality for sculpting applications by reducing distance error compared to other algorithms [S1]. Cubical marching squares and associated data structures are utilized to improve visual quality and distance error in these applications [S1]. The methods allow for efficient visual quality improvements in sculpting by providing robust inside/outside tests for vertices and samples [S1].
 
